SwirlOS - what lives in /system/apps
====================================

These .elf files are the ring-3 programs the kernel spawns:

  init.elf      session manager (spawns the shell, respawns it if it exits)
  sndtest.elf   sound self-test: 440 Hz tone at master 100 then 30, then
                an 880 Hz beep (see CHANGES-r55-audio.md). Conf-gated
                [test] sdl = sndtest, or run `sndtest` from the terminal
  shell.elf     the text shell (runs autostart.swirl, then your commands)
  desktop.elf   SwirlWM - the whole graphical desktop
  filemgr.elf   the text-mode file manager
  hello.elf     a tiny demo program

App icons (data-driven - nothing hardcoded)
-------------------------------------------

  icons/        every app icon the OS draws, as real PNG files here in
                /system/apps/icons (96x96, squircle-masked AI art):

                  folder.png file.png terminal.png about.png imgview.png
                  editor.png settings.png gl3d.png taskmgr.png doom.png
                  calc.png drvman.png nic.png netsurf.png paint.png

                At desktop init the compositor loads all of them from
                this folder, and EVERYTHING that draws an app icon -
                start menu, desktop grid, taskbar pins, window
                titlebars, Files previews - draws from here.
                Replace a PNG with your own 96x96 squircle and the
                whole OS picks it up on the next boot. If a file is
                missing, the desktop falls back to the compiled-in
                copy of the same art, so nothing ever breaks.


Built-in apps (inside desktop.elf, not separate files)
------------------------------------------------------

  Terminal      start menu, taskbar pin, `Terminal` desktop icon
  Files         start menu, taskbar pin, `Files` desktop icon
  Settings      start menu, `Settings` desktop icon, `settings` command
  3D Demo       TinyGL 0.4.1 (Fabrice Bellard) spinning square + cube,
                with a live FPS counter. Start menu, `3D Demo` desktop
                icon, or the `cube` command in the terminal.
                Keys: C = cube mode, Space = reset.
  Task Manager  live window list + kernel process table + honest memory
                panel (page-allocator numbers). Start menu, `taskmgr` or
                `top` command, `kill <pid>` / `pkill <name>` in the
                terminal.
  Editor        kilo (antirez, BSD-2) ported onto SwirlWM: typing, mouse
                caret, ctrl+a/c/x/v clipboard, ctrl+f find, ctrl+s saves
                straight to /home/user/desktop/<name>.txt. Open from the
                start menu or double-click any text file in Files.
  Image Viewer  opens .bmp files (double-click in Files)

Desktop shortcuts are real files too
------------------------------------

  The desktop grid IS the folder /home/user/desktop. Every app icon
  on it is a tiny .app file in that folder, e.g. Terminal.app with
  the single line:

      app=terminal

  Valid ids: terminal files settings gl3d taskmgr doom calc editor
  imgview drvman netsurf paint about.

  Dragging an app out of the start menu onto the desktop drops one
  of these files. Deleting the file deletes the icon; renaming it
  renames the label. Regular files saved into the folder (from the
  editor, Paint, drag & drop from Files) show up on the desktop
  next to the shortcuts automatically.

Everything on this system is a real file: this folder is a real directory
on the SwirlFS disk.
